 Pre-Release Code Review — Temporal Knowledge Graph v3                                                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                                                                 
  Methodology                                                                                                                                                                                  

  Every .go source file read from disk. Full CI pipeline executed (make ci — vet, build, test-race, gosec, govulncheck). Coverage report analyzed per-function. Zero reliance on memory or
  training data.

  ---
  File-by-File Findings

  pkg/graph/badgerstore.go

  MAJOR — badgerstore.go:137-143: FlushInterval default logic has an asymmetry with GCInterval

  flushInt := cfg.FlushInterval
  if flushInt == 0 && !cfg.InMemory {
      flushInt = defaultFlushInterval
  }
  gcInt := cfg.GCInterval
  if gcInt == 0 && !cfg.InMemory {
      gcInt = defaultGCInterval
  }

  If the user sets InMemory: false (disk mode) and explicitly passes FlushInterval: 0, this silently enables the default 100ms interval because the == 0 check can't distinguish "unset" from
  "explicitly disabled." Go's zero value for time.Duration is 0. A caller who wants manual-flush-only on a disk-backed store cannot express that. The same applies to GCInterval.

  Required fix: Use a pointer (*time.Duration) or a sentinel value (e.g., -1 to disable), or document that FlushInterval: 0 means "use default" and there's no way to disable periodic flushing
  in disk mode.

  Severity assessment: In practice, the background flush loop is always desirable in disk mode. No one would want disk-backed Badger without periodic flushing. Acceptable for production, but
  the API is misleading.

  ---
  MINOR — badgerstore.go:820-840: DeleteNodeCascade error path on getNodeLocked() failure silently succeeds

  When getNodeLocked() fails (data corruption), the cascade still deletes the node from indexes and returns nil. The caller has no idea that the node's data was unreadable. The relationships
  are cascade-deleted, the node is removed from indexes, counters adjusted — but the caller gets err == nil.

  This is a deliberate design choice (documented in lessons.md) to prevent index pollution, but returning nil on corruption is arguably wrong. Consider returning a wrapped error like "graph:
  cascade completed with corrupt node data: %w" so the caller can log or alert, while still cleaning up the indexes.

  ---
  MINOR — badgerstore.go:914: flush() at 73% coverage — all WriteBatch error recovery paths are untested

  Lines 941, 946, 956, 962 call requeueOps(ops) on WriteBatch failure. These paths are never exercised in tests. If requeueOps has a bug (e.g., it did in v3.0.10 before the map-based fix),
  failed flushes would silently lose data. Hard to test without mocking Badger, but worth noting.

  ---
  pkg/graph/wire.go

  MINOR — wire.go:251: propertyTypeTag at 56% coverage

  The uint, uint8, uint16, uint32, uint64, int8, int16, int32, float32 branches are untested. These are valid property types accepted by Set(). If a user stores uint32(42) as a property, it
  gets tagged correctly for serialization but the tag branch was never exercised in tests.

  ---
  MINOR — wire.go:385-432: toInt64 at 45.5%, toUint64 at 54.5%

  Many integer conversion branches (int, uint8, uint16, uint32 in toInt64; int8, int16, int32, int in toUint64) are uncovered. These are hit during reconstruction of lesser-used integer types.

  ---
  MINOR — wire.go:527: normalizeIntegersRecursive at 40% coverage

  The int8, int16, int32, uint8, uint16, uint32 normalization branches are untested. These are the backward-compatibility fallback for data serialized without the Type tag (ptUnknown). Old data
   would hit this path.

  ---
  pkg/graph/label_registry.go / reltype_registry.go

  MINOR — label_registry.go:145-170 / reltype_registry.go:134-159: ImportNames doesn't validate names[1:] for empty strings or duplicates

  If the persisted []string is corrupt (e.g., ["", "", "Foo"] or ["", "Foo", "Bar", "Foo"]):
  - Empty string at index 1: Lookup("") returns (1, true) while GetOrCreate("") returns ErrEmptyName — behavioral mismatch
  - Duplicate "Foo" at indexes 1 and 3: toToken["Foo"] = 3 (last wins), but Resolve(1) still returns "Foo". Queries via Lookup("Foo") would only find token 3, making entities indexed under
  token 1 invisible.

  Data-at-rest corruption scenario only — ExportNames cannot produce duplicates from normal operation. Low severity but a defensive gap.

  ---
  pkg/graph/graph.go

  MINOR — graph.go:275-308: AddRelationship generates snowflake ID before endpoint existence check

  The endpoint check happens inside store.PutRelationship(), after NextRelID(). If the endpoints don't exist, the ID is wasted. This contradicts the documented pattern "Validate before
  generating IDs" (followed for properties and type tokens). The waste is negligible (1/4096 per ms capacity) and moving the check before ID generation would require reaching into the store
  separately from PutRelationship, creating a TOCTOU window.

  Not a bug — the current design is the correct tradeoff. But the inconsistency with the documented validation order is worth acknowledging.

  ---
  pkg/graph/memorystore.go

  No issues found. Clean code, correct locking, all methods at 100% coverage except OutgoingRelationships/IncomingRelationships at 92.9% (the orphaned-index continue branch is defensive dead
  code that can't fire under the current single-RWMutex design). Acceptable.

  ---
  pkg/types/propertyslice.go

  No issues found. Recursive validation, depth limits, allowlist enforcement, nil-value handling in reflect path — all correct. 97.2% package coverage. The uncovered 2.8% is in reflectCopyValue
   (81%) for edge cases in the reflect fallback path that are hard to trigger with validated property types.

  ---
  pkg/types/node.go, relationship.go, temporal.go, integrity.go, shadow.go

  No issues found. All public methods at 100% coverage. Opaque ID types correctly wrap snowflake.ID. Constructor panics on token 0 (correct — caller error). Shared-pointer accessors documented
  as read-only. Struct alignment verified by layout_test.go.

  ---
  pkg/graph/lru.go

  No issues found. All methods at 100% coverage. Version-aware dirty tracking is correct. evictClean is O(N) single-pass. MarkFlushed correctly checks version match before clearing dirty. Clean
   tombstones are removed on flush. Thread-safe via internal mutex.

  ---
  pkg/graph/entity_locks.go

  No issues found. 100% coverage. LockTwo acquires in ascending shard order — deadlock-free. Same-shard optimization correct. Unlock in reverse order.

  ---
  pkg/graph/keys.go

  No issues found. 100% coverage. Fixed-width keys with correct byte offsets. Big-endian encoding for sort order. #nosec annotations justified.

  ---
  pkg/graph/shadow.go

  No issues found. 100% coverage. All 15 shadow keys dispatched with nil-guards on Temporal() and Integrity(). Node returns (nil, false) for tkg_type; Relationship returns (nil, false) for
  tkg_labels. Unknown keys return (nil, false).

  ---
  pkg/graph/store.go

  No issues found. Clean interface. Sentinel errors properly defined.

  ---
  pkg/graph/doc.go

  No issues found. Accurate package documentation matching the current implementation.

  ---
  Cross-Cutting Analysis

  Error Handling

  All errors checked, wrapped with context (fmt.Errorf("graph: ...: %w", err)), and propagated correctly. Sentinel errors use errors.Is in tests. Error paths in query methods correctly
  distinguish ErrNotFound (skip) from other errors (propagate). Close() returns the first error while still running all cleanup. Pass.

  Resource Management

  BadgerStore: Close() idempotent via sync.Once, stops goroutines before final flush, closes DB last. Graph: Close() saves registries before closeFn(), which always runs even on registry save
  failure. Badger iterators always Close()d after use. t.Cleanup in tests. Pass.

  Concurrency

  MemoryStore: single RWMutex, write lock for all mutations. BadgerStore: idxMu protects in-memory indexes, entityLRU.mu protects cache, wbMu protects write buffer — no lock ordering inversions
   (verified: idxMu → entityLRU.mu → wbMu in all paths). Entity lock manager prevents write-skew. Graph.closeOnce for concurrent Close safety. Race detector clean. Pass.

  Configuration Validation

  SnowflakeNodeID validated at New() by snowflake library. CacheCapacity <= 0 defaults to 10000. FlushInterval: 0 behavior documented (see issue above). All config fields are either used or
  have documented defaults. Pass (with the FlushInterval caveat).

  Dead Code, TODOs, Commented-Out Blocks

  Zero TODOs, zero FIXMEs, zero HACK comments, zero commented-out code blocks. Clean. Pass.

  Security

  No credentials or secrets in source. tkg_ prefix protection enforced at insertion. Recursive validation with allowlist (not denylist). Depth-limited recursion (32 levels). gosec clean with
  justified #nosec annotations. govulncheck clean (0 direct vulnerabilities). Property validation rejects pointers, structs, channels, functions, unsafe pointers at any nesting depth. Pass.

  Test Coverage

  92.2% graph, 97.2% types. All public methods above 0%. 543+ tests, all with t.Parallel(). Table-driven tests, mutation-independence tests, concurrency tests, boundary tests, sentinel error
  tests with errors.Is. Pass.

